WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.000
DC movies might be about superheroes&nbsp;
striving to make the world a better place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
but they're not all sunshine and daring&nbsp;
rescue -- a lot of characters, good and bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.320
don't survive. We're here to take a look at&nbsp;
their last words before they meet their maker.

00:00:12.919 --> 00:00:18.720
Superheroes are often created as an answer to&nbsp;
global unrest, rising to solve violent conflicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.560
that previously seemed insurmountable. General&nbsp;
Erich Ludendorff in 2017's Wonder Woman stands in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.000
dramatic contrast to this: he's actually based on&nbsp;
one of the real-life villains of the 20th century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.360
After contributing to significant German military&nbsp;
victories in World War I, Ludendorff became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.360
major factor in the Nazis' rise to power. He was a&nbsp;
vocal proponent of the myth which blamed Germany's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.560
defeat on home-front sabotage by multiple&nbsp;
groups, most prominently the Jewish population.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.480
Obviously, his demise in Wonder Woman is&nbsp;
well warranted. He views war as a god that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.120
bestows meaning on men, while requiring&nbsp;
sacrifice. This leads to what appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.720
to be a grudging respect for Diana, simply&nbsp;
for her power alone. At the end, he says:

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.315
"As magnificent as you are,&nbsp;
you are still no match for me."

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.840
He turns out to be dead wrong.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.080
As dangerous as General Ludendorff is to the&nbsp;
world, especially through his collaboration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.760
with Dr. Poison, the greatest threat in Wonder&nbsp;
Woman is otherworldly. Ares, the Greek god of war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.360
has been seeding destruction for a long time.&nbsp;
He implants ideas of war in the minds of humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.000
but insists that their natural corruption&nbsp;
is what actually leads to violence.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.080
Diana, in contrast, believes that the most natural&nbsp;
quality of humanity is love. As much as the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.680
might forget this, it's the most important&nbsp;
virtue, no matter what terrors pop up. As she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.960
and Ares duke it out, he tries to take advantage&nbsp;
of her grief over losing Steve Trevor. Diana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.915
asserts that she's devoted to her belief in love,&nbsp;
despite the pain of Steve's death. Ares responds:

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.229
"Then I will destroy you!"&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.800
These words are his last. Diana&nbsp;
redirects his lightning back at him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.715
just as she redirects grief into love.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.200
Steve Trevor's goodbye in Wonder Woman is&nbsp;
heartbreaking, especially since he's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.120
person who helps assure her that&nbsp;
humans have goodness inside them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.880
When Diana and Steve infiltrate a gala&nbsp;
for German High Command, she hopes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.960
confront Ludendorff, who she believes is Ares.&nbsp;
She's bewildered, then, when the general dies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.080
and the war rages on. Diana still has a lot to&nbsp;
learn about human evil at this point, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:42.320
about human goodness. Steve gives her a lesson&nbsp;
in virtue when he makes the ultimate sacrifice.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.560
When Diana attempts to stop him, he&nbsp;
refuses. He originally tells her:

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.640
"It has to be me. It has to be me. I&nbsp;
can save today. You can save the world.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.840
He regrets that they don't have more time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.400
and Diana interjects to ask what he's&nbsp;
saying. All he has left to tell her is this:

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.333
"I love you."

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.640
One of the surest signs of goodness is the ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.120
to see good in others. In his last&nbsp;
words, Steve exhibits this quality:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.080
he knows what he has to do, because he knows Diana&nbsp;
is the only one strong enough to save the world.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.080
In Man of Steel, Faora, Zod's loyal&nbsp;
second-in-command, dies on the ship that crashes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.600
into the Black Zero prison craft. This collision&nbsp;
opens a black hole that sucks Faora and the crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.720
into the Phantom Zone. Faora actually doesn't&nbsp;
speak in her final scene, and her final words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.920
in the film are a rather pedestrian few. Zod&nbsp;
gives her command of the ship, and she says:

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.323
"Faora, take command."
"Yes sir."

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.840
Not long after, Faora tries to stop&nbsp;
the last ditch effort to counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.680
Zod's plan. The hero of the day&nbsp;
winds up being Colonel Hardy.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.200
As Hardy flies Kal-El's ship to Metropolis,&nbsp;
Faora hijacks it. He realizes that the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.320
way to stop Zod is to sacrifice himself.&nbsp;
In the moments before their assumed demise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.360
Hardy repeats the words she said to him in&nbsp;
an earlier encounter. He echoes her and says:

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.630
"A good death is its own reward."

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.400
Going out the way they do&nbsp;
probably isn't what she meant.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.720
Towards the end of Man of Steel, Zod threatens to&nbsp;
destroy a family of innocents to show Superman how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.120
futile his desire to protect humanity truly is. As&nbsp;
Zod's laser vision moves closer and closer to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.480
family, he taunts the Man of Steel. Superman&nbsp;
pleads with him to stop, and Zod responds:

00:04:31.083 --> 00:04:33.543
"Stop!"

00:04:34.146 --> 00:04:36.174
"Never."

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.440
With no other choice, Superman snaps&nbsp;
his neck. When Zod dies in his arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.680
Superman breaks down and cries.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.640
Killing Zod doesn't exempt Superman from mourning:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.840
he mourns the enemy who ravaged both planets&nbsp;
Kal-El has called home. The scene is pivotal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.480
in Superman's development - having first-hand&nbsp;
experience with how it feels to take a life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.840
it's cemented in his mind that&nbsp;
he will never do so again.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:07.200
There are two major deaths in Dawn of Justice,&nbsp;
but no actual last words to speak about. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.480
he's a huge villain, Doomsday doesn't exactly&nbsp;
have a final speech, because, well, he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.120
really speak. But actions speak louder than&nbsp;
words, and threatening to destroy Superman and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:23.440
the world as we know it is a pretty substantial&nbsp;
action. So we'll count that as his final words.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.440
Superman ends this film in a coffin, though&nbsp;
considering that he comes back later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.000
his last words here aren't actually his last&nbsp;
words. But part of the significance of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:37.120
final words in Dawn of Justice comes from&nbsp;
who they're urgently spoken to. This tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.360
is evident in what he thinks will be&nbsp;
his last words, which are to Lois Lane:

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.832
"This is my world. 

00:05:48.988 --> 00:05:50.988
You are my world."

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.000
Immensely powerful stuff, considering&nbsp;
that he knows exactly what it's like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.520
lose your whole world. Luckily&nbsp;
for this world, he comes back.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:06.400
There are quite a few deaths in Suicide Squad,&nbsp;
which is no surprise, based on the movie's title.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.360
Monster T goes early when the crime boss visits&nbsp;
the Joker's club and makes the mistake of ogling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.320
Harley Quinn. When the Joker calls his girlfriend&nbsp;
over and begins taunting Monster T, the gangster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.920
quickly realizes that he's crossed a line.&nbsp;
Wanting to avoid the Joker's jealousy, he says:

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:23.680
"I don't want no beef."

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.160
But it's too late, and the Joker&nbsp;
abruptly shoots him in the head.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.520
Slipknot's last words are&nbsp;
a similarly simple tragedy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.520
Captain Boomerang claims that Amanda Waller&nbsp;
is only playing mind games with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.320
and that their heads won't actually explode if&nbsp;
they try to escape. Slipknot asks Boomerang,

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:42.299
"How do you know this?"

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.320
Mind games or not, Slipknot gets the answer to his&nbsp;
question. He doesn't survive his escape attempt.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:53.680
Finally, top Joker henchman Johnny Frost&nbsp;
says some quintessential superhero flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.400
last words before their helicopter explodes:

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.384
"Boss, we got a problem!"

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.041
He is, unfortunately, correct.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.400
Enchantress' last words in Suicide Squad&nbsp;
reflect her strong connection to her sibling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.480
and their shared power and past.&nbsp;
Initially she asks for death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.960
and she's about to have her wish granted.&nbsp;
However, Rick Flag demands that she first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.200
bring June Moone, whose body the witch has&nbsp;
used as a vessel, back to the human world.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:25.920
Rick doesn't give up the hope of saving June.&nbsp;
When he threatens to crush Enchantress' heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.880
in his hands if she doesn't bring June&nbsp;
back, Enchantress taunts him and says:

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:40.547
"She's not coming back. Go&nbsp;
ahead. You don't have the balls."

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.440
Remembering June's wish that he take&nbsp;
down the sorceress at all costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:51.760
Flag proves Enchantress wrong and crumples the&nbsp;
heart. He's delighted when the act also brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.800
June back. Enchantress' last words are,&nbsp;
however, a defiant final sentiment - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.480
fitting end for a goddess who never&nbsp;
stopped wanting to return to her throne.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:06.480
El Diablo is primed to offer the ultimate&nbsp;
sacrifice, because he's already done the emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.600
work of coming to terms with his accidental murder&nbsp;
of his family. When Enchantress telepathically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:17.520
offers to fulfill the Suicide Squad's deepest&nbsp;
desires, he alone is able to power through his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:22.800
longing for his wife and children. He's fully&nbsp;
grieved already, and so he can let them go.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:29.200
A powerhouse emotionally, physically&nbsp;
and supernaturally, El Diablo commits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.680
to a final battle to destroy Incubus.&nbsp;
As the contest progresses and he lures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.920
Incubus into the trap set by the Squad, he&nbsp;
calls out to his team to finish the job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.280
At this moment, he realizes that he has&nbsp;
to go down in order to vanquish Incubus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:49.801
once and for all. Just before the two get blown&nbsp;
up, El Diablo utters some pretty great last words:

00:08:49.801 --> 00:08:52.035
"Ya chingaste, wey."

00:08:52.035 --> 00:08:55.200
For his own part, Incubus doesn't&nbsp;
say much during the final battle;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.600
his last lines in the movie come much earlier,&nbsp;
as he seeks to restore power to his sister.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.880
"Bring me my sister's heart."

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.160
DC pulls at our heartstrings in Aquaman when we&nbsp;
see that Black Manta's origin story involves a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.840
heartbreaking goodbye between father and son.&nbsp;
Aquaman confronts Jesse Kane, the father of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:19.440
eventual Black Manta David Kane, as he attempts&nbsp;
to hijack a submarine at sea. Unlike some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:25.040
heroes, Aquaman has fewer qualms about destroying&nbsp;
villains. After he defeats Jesse and prepares to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:31.280
leave, Aquaman scolds him for setting such a&nbsp;
bad example. Kane's retaliatory shot breaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:36.640
the hull of the submarine and causes a torpedo to&nbsp;
pin him to the wall, effectively sealing his fate.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.040
Regardless of the example Jesse set for&nbsp;
David, it's Aquaman's decision to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.520
him to die that really turns the tide&nbsp;
toward vengeance in Black Manta's heart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.939
Jesse pulls the pin on a grenade so that his son&nbsp;
will leave his side and avoid drowning, saying,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.475
"Hey, you need to live so you can kill&nbsp;
that son of a b---h!

00:09:58.229 --> 00:10:01.959
 Now go. Go! ... Go."

00:10:01.959 --> 00:10:04.847
Thus, a quest for revenge is spawned.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:11.680
Sid Sivana's story in 2019's Shazam! ends&nbsp;
not in a showdown with a hero, but with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.880
own brother Thaddeus. Sid's abuses towards his&nbsp;
brother are motivated by grief and resentment:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.840
Thaddeus had, as a young boy, been selected&nbsp;
by the wizard Shazam to become his champion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.760
but was then rejected due to&nbsp;
impurity of heart. Confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.160
and hurt, Thaddeus has an angry outburst&nbsp;
while in the car with his father and brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.800
causing them to crash and his father to become&nbsp;
crippled. Sid, who goes on to rise in the ranks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.080
of their father's company, blames Thaddeus for the&nbsp;
tragedy and tortures him from that day forward.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:44.640
The last words of Thaddeus' tormentor are&nbsp;
representative of his abusive temperament.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.800
When Thaddeus, boasting newfound powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.120
shows up to a board of directors' meeting&nbsp;
to confront his father, Sid snarls:

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.818
"Don't make me throw you out of here,&nbsp;
Thad. You don't need to make a scene."

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:00.640
After this, Thad throws&nbsp;
Sid out the window instead.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:07.680
In Birds of Prey, Huntress racks up a significant&nbsp;
body count. Among her kills is Victor Zsasz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:13.040
a serial killer and employee of Roman&nbsp;
Sionis. Zsasz is paranoid and neurotic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:19.120
working with Dinah Lance, aka Black Canary, at&nbsp;
Roman's instruction, but never fully trusting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:24.480
her. Shortly before he dies, he lets loose on her&nbsp;
after discovering that local thief Cassandra Cain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:29.680
has swallowed the coveted Bertinelli Diamond,&nbsp;
saying that he knew Dinah couldn't be trusted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.770
and that Roman can only trust him. Dinah tells&nbsp;
him he's just being paranoid. He retorts:

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:43.837
"Oh, yeah? Prove it to me. Rip&nbsp;
open her little tummy. NOW!"

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.040
That's the last thing he says&nbsp;
before Huntress shoots him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:50.658
and Dinah and Harley finish the&nbsp;
job. Girls gotta stick together.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:57.600
Black Mask serves as something of a second Joker&nbsp;
to Harley Quinn: his patterns of intimidation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.560
abuse to her and the women around her nearly&nbsp;
break her. After engaging in a tense exchange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:07.360
with him as he lurks in the shadows, during&nbsp;
which Harley realizes that "Mr. J" figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:12.960
are not her protectors, but her tormentors, she&nbsp;
tries and fails to shoot Black Mask, hitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:18.080
a decoy instead. He taunts her, insinuating&nbsp;
that she doesn't stand a chance to defeat him.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.080
But Harley isn't the same Harley any longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:24.480
She's undergone major changes in the way she&nbsp;
thinks about herself and what she deserves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:28.000
Harley even tells Cassandra that she&nbsp;
made her want to be a less terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:34.268
person - pretty much the opposite of the impact&nbsp;
the Joker or Black Mask have ever had on her. Roman's reply?

00:12:34.627 --> 00:12:35.627
"Ew."

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:41.120
Not very iconic for final words, but a fitting&nbsp;
summation of the condescension Harley faces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:46.160
His final dismissal means nothing to the two&nbsp;
young women, however — especially since Cassandra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.320
has planted a bomb on him. Harley kicks the&nbsp;
villain off the bridge as the grenade detonates.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.840
Watchmen begins with Rorschach discovering a&nbsp;
terrible truth, and he eventually dies doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:02.720
same thing. He tries to warn his fellow vigilantes&nbsp;
in the interim, but they don't really take him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:08.160
seriously. Still, his quest to alert them displays&nbsp;
his doggedness. This is in contrast to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:12.960
around him: when an old compatriot ponders what&nbsp;
happened to the good old days of working together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:17.760
Rorschach dismisses him by calling him a&nbsp;
quitter, something Rorschach could never do.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:23.520
His sense of duty and obsessive personality keep&nbsp;
him on the streets, but they also alienate him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:29.520
from everyone he's ever called an ally, or even a&nbsp;
friend. His final surrender comes as a sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:35.840
in service of truth, and his devotion to it.&nbsp;
After he watches Ozymandias traumatize the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.960
he realizes the enormity&nbsp;
of what's placed upon him:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.800
he has information that could bring the world&nbsp;
crumbling down, and just as in the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:49.520
he can't commit to keeping it quiet. And so&nbsp;
he implores Doctor Manhattan to kill him.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:56.480
"Well, what are you waiting for? Do it."

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:59.760
He shouts one more time, and&nbsp;
Doctor Manhattan obliges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.156
leaving nothing but a bloody patch in the snow.

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:07.760
Though the live-action Batman films sustain&nbsp;
plenty of casualties on the villains' side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:13.040
most of them are minor characters. After all,&nbsp;
Batman's no-kill rule is central to his identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:18.640
and has deep roots in his violent origins.&nbsp;
The Joker, as a result, rarely dies in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:24.080
Batman-related media. Batman has reflected before&nbsp;
on how he can't ever kill the Joker because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:29.840
wants to so badly: he knows that if he gave into&nbsp;
that impulse, he might not be able to pull back.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:34.400
The death of Jack Nicholson's&nbsp;
Joker in 1989's Batman is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.360
appropriately enough, preceded by these words:

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:41.200
"Sometimes I just kill myself."

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:46.080
It's a demise of his own design: all Batman does&nbsp;
is tether him to a gargoyle, and the Joker's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:51.680
attempted escape leads to his fatal fall.&nbsp;
Batman keeps to his code ... sort of. As we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:55.760
see in the next moment, when he saves himself&nbsp;
and Vicki Vale from plummeting to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.760 --> 00:15:00.720
Batman has another grappling hook on him.&nbsp;
Does that count in violating his rule?

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:06.160
Time and again, Batman finds himself&nbsp;
dangling from a deadly height,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:09.120
only for his enemies to meet&nbsp;
their end at the bottom instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:14.560
Two-Face suffers this grisly fate in Batman&nbsp;
Forever. It's a bit hard to tell if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:19.360
was an accident: when Two-Face flips his coin at&nbsp;
Batman's suggestion, the hero flusters the villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:24.640
by tossing a bunch of decoy coins into the air.&nbsp;
Not inherently a lethal strike, but considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:30.160
that Two-Face was balancing on a metal beam at&nbsp;
the time, what else did Bats expect to happen?

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.880
As Two-Face prepares to shoot Batman and his crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:35.680
Batman reminds him that he&nbsp;
should let the coin decide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:40.560
since he's always of two minds about everything.&nbsp;
Two-Face has an impressive response for him:

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:44.607
"Emotion is always the enemy of true justice.&nbsp;
Thank you. You've always been a good friend."

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:49.520
Some poignant last words to the man&nbsp;
who sort-of-kind-of kills him. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:54.160
it's a pretty perfect line, considering that&nbsp;
it's knowing Harvey Dent so well as a friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.760
and foe that allows the Dark&nbsp;
Knight to best him one last time.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.600
Bane appears to have every advantage&nbsp;
against Batman in The Dark Knight Rises.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:06.800
His goal is to destroy Gotham&nbsp;
and force Batman to watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:12.480
only allowing him the mercy of death once he's&nbsp;
seen everything he loves go up in flames. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:18.480
vengeful mass homicide is complicated. Eventually,&nbsp;
it becomes inconvenient to keep Batman alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:22.720
even though the final stages haven't yet&nbsp;
been executed. This change of plan is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:28.320
a small sacrifice for Bane, who doesn't seem&nbsp;
overwhelmingly bothered when he tells Batman,

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:30.720
"We both know I have to kill you now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.058
You'll just have to imagine the fire."

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:38.720
But that never happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:42.880
because he's shot in the chest by Selina&nbsp;
Kyle operating the Batpod's cannon.

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.560
The image of Gotham burning is a&nbsp;
bitter threat to this film's Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:51.040
who has watched his name burn for&nbsp;
eight years to keep Gotham standing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:55.760
But Bats will weather the fires of the city's&nbsp;
hatred forever, if it means that Gotham is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.760 --> 00:17:02.400
safe from the flames. This strength is one that&nbsp;
Bane can never match, and never completely kill.

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:08.720
Ra's al Ghul's death in Batman Begins reflects&nbsp;
Batman's ongoing, internal struggle between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:13.920
good and evil. The villain was once Batman's&nbsp;
savior and mentor — without him, there would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:20.960
be no Batman at all. And yet, he's corrupt, and&nbsp;
Batman has to make his peace with that, if he can.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:25.440
The two adversaries brawl on a runaway train&nbsp;
transporting a microwave emitter that will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:30.800
used against Gotham. Ra's holds the upper hand&nbsp;
on his foe, but eventually Batman takes control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:35.040
Ra's still taunts our hero when it looks&nbsp;
like Batman is going to kill him and asks,

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.578
"Have you finally learned&nbsp;
to do what is necessary?"

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:41.600
Batman replies that he wouldn't take Ra's' life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:44.800
but he also doesn't have to&nbsp;
save him. In his last moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:51.360
Ra's realizes that Batman is indeed equipped&nbsp;
to win against injustice — injustice like him.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:56.400
Batman is simply doing what Ra's taught&nbsp;
him: he's devoting himself to an ideal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:01.676
Batman's commitment to compassion is alien&nbsp;
to Ra's.  The League of Shadows is a bit more... 

00:18:01.676 --> 00:18:06.812
Uh... Let's just say not compassionate when it comes to... well... everything.  

00:18:06.812 --> 00:18:10.480
This devotion&nbsp;
makes Bruce more than an ordinary man in a cape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:14.560
as Ra's claims at the start of their&nbsp;
throwdown - it makes him legendary.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:20.480
Though the main villains in Batman v. Superman:&nbsp;
Dawn of Justice weren't human, there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:25.520
certainly a couple of earthly adversaries who&nbsp;
made things difficult for the good guys. For part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:30.320
of the film, two of the most vicious of these,&nbsp;
African terrorist General Amajagh and Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:35.840
mercenary Anatoli Knyazev, were working together,&nbsp;
and their last words have a similar ring to them.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:41.360
Though he didn't have as big of a role in this&nbsp;
film, Knyazev is the alter ego of a DC Comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:46.720
supervillain known as KGBeast. Fans are hoping&nbsp;
that somehow or other, the character will get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:51.280
another chance in the DC Cinematic Universe&nbsp;
that explores the breadth of his presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:56.640
the comics: Multiple people have pointed out that&nbsp;
the original character is cybernetically enhanced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:01.120
and that perhaps these modifications could&nbsp;
have been a result of the explosion that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:04.800
at least as far as Batman v.&nbsp;
Superman viewers know, killed him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:09.055
Perhaps if his last words hadn't been&nbsp;
so threatening, he might have lived.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.264
"I'll kill her! Believe me, I'll do it!"

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:17.801
In his final moments, the warlord Amajagh attempts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.801 --> 00:19:21.200
to put Superman in a bind by&nbsp;
holding Lois Lane hostage:

00:19:21.889 --> 00:19:25.266
"Take one step. You will&nbsp;
see the inside of her head."

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:30.880
Of course, Superman doesn't need to take any&nbsp;
steps at all when he can fly at supersonic speeds:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:35.040
He rams Amajagh through the wall before&nbsp;
he has the chance to fire the shot.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:39.920
While it certainly seems like General Amajagh&nbsp;
meets his end at the Man of Steel's hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:45.760
director Zack Snyder left things ambiguous in a&nbsp;
text posted on Reddit, which stated Amajagh was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:51.840
quote, "not dead but not a problem either."&nbsp;
Either way, we won't be hearing from him again.

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:57.760
In a superhero universe, dead or alive&nbsp;
status can get a little confusing. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:02.560
when a character exits the scene for good,&nbsp;
you can usually pick up on the intent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:08.080
and the old Wizard Shazam dissolving into dust&nbsp;
after transferring his powers to Billy Batson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:13.280
is as good an indication as any. Shazam&nbsp;
lives on, but the old wizard is gone.

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:18.480
Plus, his last words come in the form of an&nbsp;
extremely quotable speech, give background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:23.920
on the magic and age-old conflicts at play, and&nbsp;
lay the groundwork for Billy to become the hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:28.720
he is meant to be. After explaining that his&nbsp;
brothers and sisters have all been wiped out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:34.400
Shazam finally convinces the young boy to&nbsp;
speak his name and become the new Champion.

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:37.887
"Carry my name, and with&nbsp;
it you carry all my power."

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.080
After that, Billy gets to skip&nbsp;
puberty and become a deep-voiced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:48.560
deified superhero. Just before the wizard crumbles&nbsp;
into dust, he offers one more nugget of wisdom:

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:54.800
"With your heart, unlock your greatest power.&nbsp;
The thrones of our brothers and sisters await!"

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:59.760
One of the first victims of Ozymandias'&nbsp;
scheme to unite the world by manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:04.480
a nuclear threat is a vigilante known as&nbsp;
the Comedian. With vigilantism outlawed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:08.960
a few former vigilantes like the Comedian&nbsp;
became government agents instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:13.440
and when he learned of Ozymandias'&nbsp;
plans, Ozymandias has him killed.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:17.840
By this time, though, the Comedian is beyond&nbsp;
disillusioned with the state of the world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:23.120
the relative powerlessness of any one person&nbsp;
to combat the injustice inherent in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:28.160
By the time he meets his end, The Comedian&nbsp;
had already had his last laugh long ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:33.360
seeing life, and the power structures that&nbsp;
control everything we do, as a huge joke.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:35.280
Though he fights his assailant to the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:39.440
there's a dull note of defeat in his&nbsp;
voice when he offers his final words:

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:45.760
"It's a joke. It's all a&nbsp;
joke. Mother, forgive me."

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:51.360
In the end, though he had fought for justice all&nbsp;
his life as a vigilante, what he really wanted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:55.920
himself was forgiveness for the injustices&nbsp;
he himself chose to commit along the way.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:02.720
Since Rorschach discovers him dead, Moloch's last&nbsp;
words on film come significantly before his actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:08.720
demise must have occurred. In a previous meeting&nbsp;
between the two men. Rorschach confronts Moloch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:13.120
a retired supervillain, at his apartment,&nbsp;
demanding to know why Moloch attended the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:18.240
funeral of the Comedian's civilian alter ego —&nbsp;
and how Moloch knew they were the same person.

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:21.120
Moloch's account details an&nbsp;
encounter with a drunken and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:24.880
unmasked Comedian who invaded&nbsp;
Moloch's bedroom late at night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:29.040
Moloch feared that he was there to kill&nbsp;
him, but the Comedian just wanted to talk.

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:33.760
Seeing that Rorschach is satisfied enough with&nbsp;
his story, Moloch asks if things are settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:39.200
between them. Rorschach rummages through one&nbsp;
of his drawers and finds an illegal. Moloch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:44.320
begs him not to confiscate it, saying that&nbsp;
he's trying any treatment for his cancer.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.395
"You know the kind you eventually get&nbsp;
better from? 

00:22:49.121 --> 00:22:50.893
That ain't the kind I got."

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:55.680
The 2012 final installment of Christopher&nbsp;
Nolan's Batman trilogy, The Dark Knight Rises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.680 --> 00:23:00.960
finds Talia al Ghul carrying on her father's&nbsp;
legacy as one of Batman's chief adversaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:04.720
though she doesn't reveal herself&nbsp;
as such to begin with. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:09.520
she elects to have Bane do the dirty work&nbsp;
for her, making it his "personal mission" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:15.520
destroy Gotham as her father wanted while Talia&nbsp;
masquerades as Bruce's confidante and lover.

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:20.960
But if you want something done right, eventually&nbsp;
you really do have to do it yourself. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:26.960
why when Batman manages to overpower Bane, Talia&nbsp;
steps in, stabbing the caped crusader. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:30.720
it's pretty easy to figure out that&nbsp;
she's not who she said she was, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:36.880
she helpfully reveals her identity as Ra's al&nbsp;
Ghul's daughter just in case anyone was confused.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:42.000
Talia has been working to finish what her father&nbsp;
started ever since his death. All she cared about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:46.560
was vengeance for her father's death and&nbsp;
the realization of his destructive goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:49.200
and her last words pay tribute to her life as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:52.640
remotely floods the reactor chamber&nbsp;
that has stabilized an atomic bomb:

00:23:52.927 --> 00:23:57.760
"My father's work is done."

